The vulnerability in this code is that it allows for arbitrary HTML to be injected into the page without sanitization or escaping. This can lead to cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks if an attacker can control the value of `location.hash`.

To fix this, you should use a library like DOMPurify to sanitize the input before inserting it into the DOM:

```javascript
import { sanitize } from 'dompurify';

const sanitizedHtml = sanitize(location.hash.slice(1));
element.innerHTML = sanitizedHtml;
```

By using DOMPurify or another similar library, you ensure that any potentially malicious HTML is stripped out and only safe content is rendered. This helps prevent XSS attacks by ensuring that the code does not execute arbitrary JavaScript or render unsafe HTML elements.
